"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,754\nTeamwork: how a memory moves through the brain\n\n3\n00:00:09,754 --> 00:00:17,958\nIn 1953, a surgeon removed most of the hippocampus on both sides of the brain of a 27-year-old man,\n\n4\n00:00:18,034 --> 00:00:21,022\nHenry Molaison, to control his seizures.\n\n5\n00:00:21,477 --> 00:00:26,220\nAfterwards, Henry could no longer form new lasting memories of events.\n\n6\n00:00:26,581 --> 00:00:28,657\nYet he still remembered his childhood.\n\n7\n00:00:29,074 --> 00:00:32,325\nThose old memories must have been stored somewhere else.\n\n8\n00:00:34,495 --> 00:00:37,997\nThe hippocampus sits deep inside each temporal lobe.\n\n9\n00:00:38,514 --> 00:00:40,088\nWhen something happens to you,\n\n10\n00:00:40,302 --> 00:00:42,957\ndifferent parts of the cortex handle the sights,\n\n11\n00:00:43,175 --> 00:00:44,742\nthe sounds and the meaning.\n\n12\n00:00:45,265 --> 00:00:48,482\nThe hippocampus links those pieces into one episode.\n\n13\n00:00:50,652 --> 00:00:51,451\nIt has help.\n\n14\n00:00:51,962 --> 00:00:55,229\nThe amygdala marks emotional moments as important.\n\n15\n00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:59,173\nThe dopamine system flags surprise and reward.\n\n16\n00:00:59,453 --> 00:01:02,471\nAnd the prefrontal cortex helps organize memories,\n\n17\n00:01:02,478 --> 00:01:03,924\nand later search for them.\n\n18\n00:01:05,094 --> 00:01:07,198\nMuch of the moving happens during sleep.\n\n19\n00:01:07,711 --> 00:01:15,640\nIn rats, hippocampal cells that fired together as the animal explored fired together again in the deep sleep that followed.\n\n20\n00:01:16,207 --> 00:01:19,889\nIn people who learned card locations while smelling roses,\n\n21\n00:01:20,161 --> 00:01:24,199\nthe same scent during deep sleep improved their memory the next day.\n\n22\n00:01:24,369 --> 00:01:25,779\nOver weeks to years,\n\n23\n00:01:25,979 --> 00:01:28,343\nlinks within the cortex grow stronger,\n\n24\n00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:31,503\nand many memories depend less on the hippocampus.\n\n25\n00:01:31,956 --> 00:01:34,491\nThis is called systems consolidation,\n\n26\n00:01:34,795 --> 00:01:37,844\nand it may be why Henry kept his older memories.\n\n27\n00:01:38,293 --> 00:01:41,341\nResearchers still debate how complete the move is,\n\n28\n00:01:41,556 --> 00:01:44,239\nespecially for vivid personal memories.\n\n29\n00:01:44,409 --> 00:01:46,868\nMemories are not filed away for good.\n\n30\n00:01:47,272 --> 00:01:51,560\nIn rats, a fear memory that was recalled became fragile again,\n\n31\n00:01:51,726 --> 00:01:53,209\nand had to be stored again.\n\n32\n00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:55,648\nEach time you remember something,\n\n33\n00:01:55,751 --> 00:01:57,677\nthe memory can be strengthened,\n\n34\n00:01:57,697 --> 00:01:58,544\nor changed.\n\n35\n00:02:00,215 --> 00:02:01,816\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n36\n00:02:02,196 --> 00:02:04,487\nMemory is teamwork that takes time.\n\n37\n00:02:04,878 --> 00:02:06,160\nSpread out your study,\n\n38\n00:02:06,204 --> 00:02:08,469\nget enough sleep, and practice recalling,\n\n39\n00:02:08,544 --> 00:02:11,312\nso each memory is rebuilt and strengthened.\n\n40\n00:02:12,982 --> 00:02:15,118\nA memory starts with the hippocampus.\n\n41\n00:02:15,456 --> 00:02:16,678\nWith sleep and time,\n\n42\n00:02:16,698 --> 00:02:18,654\nthe whole team helps it last.\n\n"
